$\underbrace{\sqrt{~\overbrace{4x+1}^{\text{inner}}~}}_{\text{outer}}$

So if $\sqrt{4x+1}=w(u(x))$, then:

$\begin{align}
\goldD{u(x)}&=\goldD{4x+1} &&\text{inner function}
\\\\
w(x)&=\sqrt{x}&&\text{outer function}
\end{align}$

Before applying the chain rule, let's find the derivatives of the inner and outer functions (work not shown, but hopefully these derivatives are familiar by now).

$\begin{align}
\maroonD{u'(x)}&=\maroonD{4}
\\\\
\blueD{w'(x)}&=\blueD{\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt{x}}}
\end{align}$

Now let's apply the chain rule:

$\begin{align}
\dfrac{dy}{dx}&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left[w\Bigl(u(x)\Bigr)\right]
\\\\
&=\blueD{w'\Bigl(\goldD{u(x)}\Bigr)}\cdot\maroonD{u'(x)}
\\\\
&=\blueD{\dfrac{1}{2\sqrt{(\goldD{4x+1})}}} \cdot \maroonD{4}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{2}{\sqrt{4x+1}}
\end{align}$
